    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90562.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, there is. And, you know, I've experienced it because when I got elected to Congress after 40 years, we turned it to Republican control. We elected Newt Gingrich. Now, Newt Gingrich, I remember after the second election where I went to Congress, and it was tough. My district that I represented was 16% Republican, 1-6, not 6-0. And Gingrich was being attacked, and Newt actually personally to my face said, go ahead and attack me. And I said, I don't have to do that. I mentioned that because I got through the election. The phone rang. I remember on a Thursday in St. Clair's, Ohio, and it was Newt Gingrich himself. And he said, are you going to vote for me? For speaker. And I said, you know, I'm taken back by this. And he said, I'm going to ask you again, are you going to vote for me? I said, now I'm angry. Why would you say that? He said there's about a few members leading this charge. And if I lose six votes, I'm not speaker. Now, I watched that unfold. The first Republican speaker in 40 years, and I bring this up, Kevin, because if there was ever a moment where an ego could step in and say, well, wait a minute, I'm not letting you do this. It would have been Gingrich, because he carried out the Republican Revolution, the contract with America. It was he who got that Congress converted. And you know what he did? He just said, OK, I remember being in a room. He said, if you don't want me, you don't want me. I understand. I'm going to leave. That is rare. Normally,",
