  {
    "episode_id": 90562,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90562.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 35,
    "turn_text": "Ohio doesn't really know J.C. Vance. I served with Tim Ryan. Tim Ryan ran against J.D. Vance. I knew Josh Mandel. I mentioned Josh because he was the state treasurer at one time, very aggressive, very popular. A young upstart that just came out of nowhere and he was taking Ohio politics by storm. He was ahead in the Senate race. I had no doubt Josh Mandel was going to win. right out of nowhere came JD Vance, who was from Middletown, Ohio. I'm from Appalachia down across from West Virginia. He's from Middletown, Ohio. He wrote Hillbilly Elegy, a move to California. I think they had a residence in DC, Virginia, and he became not super wealthy. He doesn't have Bill Gates' wealth, but he became seven or eight million wealthy off of these investors. He got to know Peter Thiel, so he became an entrepreneur. Then all of a sudden Donald Trump latches onto him because Peter Thiel, who I don't think supports Trump anymore, but supported him at the time and called for JD Vance, Trump comes in Ohio, JD Vance wins the nomination. JD Vance, up to June, as I understand it, from political managers here, the Republican side, wasn't even doing weekend events. Right. He had never been in politics. So we kind of look at him as where did this come from? Then he beat Tim Ryan, who was a great great candidate, by the way, and he beat him. And all of a sudden we had a senator who was getting very close to Trump as days went on. It was obvious he was gearing himself towards Trump. Now, if we go back over all the things he ever said about Trump, it was the toughest of anybody.",
